    <h1>What Is a Group of Ravens Called?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p>A group of ravens is most commonly called an <strong>"unkindness"</strong> or a <strong>"conspiracy"</strong>. "Unkindness" has the older historical record, appearing in English sources from the 15th century. "Conspiracy" is a modern term in widespread popular use. Neither is a strict scientific term — in ornithology, the word used is simply "flock".</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Historical Record: "Unkindness"</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The collective noun "unkindness of ravens" appears in <em>The Book of St Albans</em> (1486), one of the earliest printed English books, which compiled lists of collective nouns for animals used in hunting and heraldry. The same work gives us "a murder of crows" and "a parliament of owls".<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>These medieval collective nouns were largely literary inventions — poetic or alliterative phrases rather than terms in common everyday use. "Unkindness" may reflect the raven's reputation as a bird of ill omen, or its aggressive behaviour when competing for food.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>"Conspiracy" — a Modern Addition</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> "Conspiracy of ravens" is not documented in historical sources with the same antiquity as "unkindness". It appears in modern popular books, wildlife websites, and social media from the late 20th century onwards. It has become widely used and is now found in many reference lists, but its origin is recent and unclear.</p>
    <p>Both terms are used by English speakers today; neither is incorrect. If you need the historically attested term, use "unkindness".</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Ravens Actually Do in Groups</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Young, non-breeding Common Ravens (typically 1–3 year olds) form communal roosts that can reach hundreds or even thousands of birds, particularly in winter near large food sources. Adults with established territories rarely join these flocks — they roost with their partner in their territory.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Bernd Heinrich's research (<em>Ravens in Winter</em>, 1989) showed that ravens use communal roosts as "information centres": a raven that failed to find food at night will follow successful foragers from the roost in the morning. The group benefits because many birds searching a wide area find more food than any individual could alone.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>At large carcasses, groups of ravens will gather and can be noisy and competitive. This social feeding behaviour — not any conspiratorial intent — is likely what gave the birds their dramatic collective nouns.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Scientific Term: Flock</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> In ornithology and scientific writing, a group of ravens is called a <strong>flock</strong>. Scientific literature does not use "unkindness" or "conspiracy" — these are literary and popular terms. The distinction matters if you are reading or writing scientific material about ravens.</p>
  </section>

